[02:12.52]W: Hi Alan, sorry for not calling you back sooner.
[02:16.38]W: You said on the phone you had something you needed to discuss with me?
[02:20.02]M: Well, to get right down to it, I'm having second thoughts about my major.
[02:25.50]M: With my family situation getting more complicated, and the end of my university education nearing, persisting with art history just doesn't seem the right choice.
[02:36.60]W: Alan, in the last three years, I have heard nothing but how passionate you are about the art history program.
[02:44.68]W: You often told fellow classmates how fabulous the instructors were, and that you finally felt accepted.
[02:51.26]W: What would cause you to abandon it?
[02:54.08]M: My father's current financial situation has helped me realize that a more financially viable employment opportunity is not necessarily a bad thing.
[03:04.68]M: Art history nourishes my soul, but does nothing to put food on my plate.
[03:11.86]W: What about all the work opportunities I discussed with you last month?
[03:15.94]W: Have you gone to those places of business to investigate whether any legitimate positions might be available for someone with your background?
[03:24.82]M: Um, well...
[03:25.38]W: Look, Alan, if your heart is set on working in the art industry, you need to be more assertive and start talking with people.
[03:33.52]W: Networking is key if you want to become a celebrity in the world of museums and galleries.
[03:39.96]M: Time is a very precious commodity right now.
[03:42.84]M: My social life does not exist.
[03:45.26]M: I can barely keep up with my studies and thesis is due by the end of this year.
[03:51.56]M: When can I find time to research jobs when I have so much to do as it is?
[03:56.72]W: What you do now will determine your future options.
[04:00.90]W: Now is the optimum time to put forth your best effort.
[04:05.54]M: I will take what you said into consideration.
[04:08.94]M: The advice is much appreciated.
[04:17.90]Q1. What did the man want to discuss with the woman?
[04:35.20]Q2. Why does the man want to give up art history?
[04:52.68]Q3. What does the woman advise the man to do?
[05:10.22]Q4. What does the man say he really lacks right now?
[05:28.98]M: Have you decided yet what your end of semester business project will be on?
[05:33.48]W: Yes, how about you?
[05:35.84]M: Yes, go on.
[05:37.76]M: You go first.
[05:39.10]W: I'm going to do it about beef.
[05:41.82]M: Beef?
[05:42.38]W: Yeah, beef.
[05:43.94]W: I'm going to design a business case study for a cow farm that supplies beef for the domestic market.
[05:51.00]M: Wow, that sounds very interesting and original.
[05:54.62]M: Where ever did you get that idea?
[05:56.26]W: I don't know, it just came to me I guess.
[05:59.22]W: But think about it.
[06:00.14]W: Beef consumption has been rising strongly over the past 20 years.
[06:04.90]W: As the economy continues to grow, consumer demand is expected to continue to increase, for the foreseeable future.
[06:12.48]W: And currently, most of the beef is imported, which brings with it added fees.
[06:17.90]W: So why not raise cattle here?
[06:20.56]M: Of course.
[06:22.02]M: Makes perfect sense.
[06:23.58]M: There's plenty of land out west, so if done right, it should be very profitable to raise our own cattle.
[06:31.34]W: Exactly.
[06:33.10]M: Seriously, that sounds like a great idea.
[06:36.04]M: You should genuinely look into it once we get our degrees.
[06:40.06]W: Thanks.
[06:40.94]W: So, your turn now.
[06:42.58]W: What are you doing your case study on?
[06:45.02]M: I was thinking about a car wash, but it now seems like such a boring notion after hearing your brilliant idea.
[06:51.68]M: That's cool.
[06:52.48]M: I could see that working.
[06:53.50]M: After all, there's plenty of cars about, and those aren't going away anytime soon.
[06:59.80]M: While many people don't care how their car looks, many other people do.
[07:04.32]M: You've got yourself a vast and continual market.
[07:07.70]W: Right.
[07:09.12]W: So the idea is a large, self cleaning car wash.
[07:12.98]W: But I mean really big and well outfitted, like they have in America.
[07:17.30]W: I was thinking of having it cater to high end consumers in large cities.
[07:21.62]W: It could charge a profitable mark up by offering just a bit more technical equipment and professional service than competitors.
