[09:15.68]Increased television time for young children has long been linked with poorer progress in some areas of development.
[09:23.68]One possible way to counter those negative effects is talking while watching the screen.
[09:29.50]As a new study suggests, the more parents engaged in conversation with preschoolers during TV time, the more likely those children were to have higher curiosity levels when they reached kindergarten.
[09:42.80]This was particularly true for children with socioeconomic disadvantages.
[09:49.28]"Our findings reinforce the importance of parent conversation to promote early childhood development and curiosity," said lead author Prachi Shah at University of Michigan Health C.S. Mott Children's Hospital.
[10:04.46]Researchers assessed hours of daily television exposure and frequency of parent screen time conversation among 1,500 preschoolers.
[10:14.64]And then measured early childhood curiosity in kindergarten.
[10:19.20]The study specifically focused on curiosity levels, which are associated with enhanced learning and higher academic achievement in reading and math at kindergarten.
[10:29.96]And behavioral developmental benefits, especially for children from families with lower socioeconomic status.
[10:38.22]While many young children are now growing up with digital media exposure through mobile devices like tablets and phones.
[10:45.66]Television remains a dominant screen activity, accounting for 72% of all screen time.
[10:53.64]TVs are in 98% of all homes, keeping television exposure a relevant developmental context in young children.
[11:02.86]Excessive media exposure, including television, can displace exploratory activities such as play and parent child interactions, which are believed to be key to cultivating curiosity in kids.
[11:15.92]Our findings suggest the importance of parents finding opportunities to foster conversational exchanges in daily routines with their young children, including while watching television, Shah said.
[11:37.16]Q9. What do we learn from a new study about young children?
[11:55.34]Q10. What did Prachi Shah's study specifically focus on?
[12:13.46]Q11. What does the passage say about excessive media exposure?
[12:34.18]Humans are aspirational, goal seeking beings.
[12:38.08]Each of us, according to research, has an inborn drive to acquire.
[12:41.78]The dark side of this drive to acquire is being mindlessly driven to possess more and more things and to socially compare our stuff with all the stuff other people have in their lives.
[12:57.86]So the question is, how do we ensure we tap into our drive to acquire in positive ways that will benefit our well being?
[13:08.54]One way is to become more intentionally aspirational.
[13:13.36]To be intentionally aspirational includes consciously aspiring to be the person you need to be to succeed in your professional and personal life.
[13:23.38]It also includes consciously aspiring to acquire things in your life that will positively impact your sense of well being.
[13:32.44]If you're not consciously, mindfully, and intentionally harnessing your inborn drive to acquire, you might be mindlessly pursuing all the wrong things.
[13:46.56]While your intentional, aspirational goals will include the pursuit of acquiring things that will increase your sense of well being, they ought to also include goals to help other people in your various life roles.
[14:02.64]Your intentional, aspirational goals will also include striving to be your personal best, a person of good character, integrity, and trustworthiness.
[14:14.54]Think about this from a practical rather than theoretical viewpoint.
[14:20.16]If you're not pursuing intentional aspirational goals, you have nothing to strive for.
[14:26.72]If you have nothing to strive for, you're putting yourself in the dangerous position of simply drifting through life, and you'll not be able to experience, live, and enjoy all that life has to offer.
[14:53.28]Q12. What is the dark side of our inborn drive to acquire?
[15:08.46]Q13. What might happen if we do not harness our inborn drive to acquire?
[15:27.58]Q14. What does the passage say about our intentional aspirational goals?
[15:47.14]Q15. What will you end up doing without pursuing intentional aspirational goals?
[16:34.48]It's revision time for exams and dissertations.
[16:38.36]The pressure is on, so you'll want all the help you can get to age your memory and raise your grades.
[16:45.38]As a nutrition expert, I want to say that eating well can make a real difference to your revision regime.
[16:52.88]So, what brain boosting food and drink would I recommend?
[16:57.40]Coffee can have numerous benefits.
[16:59.76]However, while coffee may make you more alert, Individuals can build up a tolerance, meaning this is short lived.
[17:08.54]Coffee can also increase blood sugar and eventually cause lack of focus and energy.
[17:14.28]It's also worth noting that people react differently to coffee.
[17:17.78]Some people can drink it at midnight and go straight to sleep, whereas others get feelings of anxiety after a small amount.
[17:27.34]Also remember that coffee from a coffee shop may be stronger than coffee made at home, so stop drinking coffee by 2 p.
[17:35.70]m.
[17:36.20]and have a maximum of two cups of coffee a day.
[17:39.98]But be aware of your own reaction to it.
[17:44.14]Whole grain foods will ward off hunger.
[17:48.08]Examples include porridge and whole wheat bread.
[17:51.80]Combining whole grain foods with protein will help keep your blood sugar levels balanced, which is essential for mood and concentration.
[18:01.82]Berries and nuts are a convenient and nutritious snack.
[18:05.04]Blueberries, like many dark colored fruits and vegetables, can slow down brain damage and age-related decline.
[18:14.26]Frozen berries are also a good choice.
[18:17.16]They are usually cheap, last longer, and don't lose their nutrients when frozen.
[18:23.34]But flavored and coated nuts are less healthy, as they contain added oil, salt, and sugar.
[18:31.30]Doctors often recommend taking vitamin supplements to top up on the nutrients you need.
[18:37.18]But I would say food should always come before supplements, and the key to getting as many nutrients as possible is to eat a varied diet, with lots of different colors such as frozen berries and dark green vegetables.
[18:51.52]It's better to get everything you need from food and drink.
[18:55.64]For example, oranges contain not only vitamin C, which boosts the immune system, but also fiber and other components that you can't get packaged together in a tablet.
[19:07.78]Don't forget to drink a proper amount of water, which can boost attention by almost 25%, and can elevate your mood as well.
[19:17.22]Finally, don't skip meals.
[19:19.74]Eating regularly will help keep blood sugar balanced, and feed the brain with the fuel it needs.
[19:34.20]Q16. What does the speaker recommend in this talk?
[19:52.42]Q17. What does the speaker warn against about coffee?
