[20:31.24]A late Swedish academic has identified a worrying trend.
[20:37.32]Not only do many people across advanced economies have no idea that the world is becoming a much better place, but they actually even think the opposite.
[20:49.10]This is no wonder, when the news focuses on reporting catastrophes, terrorist attacks, wars, and famines.
[20:58.76]Who wants to hear about the fact that every day some 200,000 people around the world are lifted above the two dollar a day poverty line?
[21:08.82]Or that more than 300,000 people get access to electricity and clean water for the first time every day?
[21:17.60]These stories of people in low income countries simply don't make for exciting news coverage.
[21:24.82]But as Rosling pointed out in his book Factfulness.
[21:29.42]It's important to put all the bad news in perspective.
[21:33.84]While it is true that globalization has put some downward pressure on middle class wages in advanced economies in recent decades, it has also helped lift hundreds of millions of people out of poverty.
[21:50.76]If we really care about global welfare, globalization is the only way forward to ensure that economic prosperity is shared among all countries and not only a select few advanced economies.
[22:06.86]While some people glorify the past, One of the big facts of economic history is that until quite recently, a significant part of the world's population has lived under quite miserable conditions.
[22:19.69]And this is true throughout most of human history.
[22:24.84]While there were many long lasting ups and downs, like the Great Depression or the recent Great Recession, the constancy of the long run growth rate is actually quite miraculous.
[22:38.36]Low income countries, including China and India, have been growing at a significantly faster pace in recent decades, and are quickly catching up to the West.
[22:49.62]A 10 percent growth rate over a prolonged period means that income levels double roughly every seven years.
[22:57.24]It is obviously good news if prosperity is more shared across the globe.
[23:04.96]While inequality within countries has gone up as a result of globalization, global inequality has been on a steady downward trend for several decades.
[23:16.98]This is mostly a result of the improvement of hundreds of millions of people's living standards.
[23:23.84]In fact, for the first time ever since the Industrial Revolution, about half of the global population can be considered middle class.
[23:41.56]Q19. What is the worrying trend the late Swedish academic has identified?
[24:00.88]Q20. What does the speaker say about globalization?
[24:18.84]Q21. What do we learn about half of the global population?
[24:38.02]We all know that the sense of smell is powerful.
[24:41.38]The delicious scent of bread, for example, can act like a time machine.
[24:45.86]One sniff can bring back a memory from many years ago.
[24:49.82]Indeed, scent particles, in general, can revive memories that have been long forgotten.
[24:56.44]But why do smells sometimes trigger powerful memories, especially emotional ones?
[25:02.52]The short answer is that the brain regions that handle smells, memories, and emotions are linked.
[25:09.82]A scent is a chemical particle that floats in through the nose and into the region of the brain responsible for smell, where the sensation is first processed into a form that the brain can read.
[25:22.58]Brain cells then carry that information to the area of the brain where emotions are processed, and then to an adjacent area of the brain where learning and memory formation take place.
[25:35.52]Sense are the only sensations that travel such a direct path to the emotional and memory centers of the brain.
[25:41.68]That results in an intimate connection between emotions, memories, and sense.
[25:47.92]Which is why memories triggered by sense are experienced as more emotional than those triggered by sight or sound.
[25:56.10]Let's take a closer look at how those memories are activated.
[26:00.88]Usually, when a person smells something that's connected to a meaningful event in their past, they'll first have an emotional response to the sensation, and then a memory might follow.
[26:12.86]But sometimes, the memory doesn't come to the surface.
[26:16.32]The person might feel the emotion of something that happened in the past, but won't remember what they experienced.
[26:22.88]What causes this baffling phenomenon?
[26:25.96]The explanation is context.
[26:28.70]Imagine this scenario.
[26:30.38]A person is walking down the street, smelling a scent that they first encountered decades ago, which activates an emotional response.
[26:39.52]If the person had first come across that smell in a very different context, it will be much more difficult to recover the associated memory, because the brain uses the context to give meaning to the information and find that memory.
[26:53.88]The special nature of memories brought back by scents notwithstanding, they have the same drawbacks as other memories.
[27:01.22]They aren't always accurate, and they can change over time.
[27:05.18]In fact, each time we remember something, that memory can become distorted.
[27:11.02]But, because of the strong emotional associations these memories evoke, people who remember something due to a scent are often convinced that the memories are accurate, even when they aren't.
[27:34.64]Q22. What question does the speaker try to answer in this talk?
[27:50.38]Q23. What is said to result in an intimate connection between emotions, memories, and sense?
[28:13.00]Q24. What does the brain use to give meaning to information and find a memory?
[28:33.76]Q25. What drawback do memories of sense have in common with other memories?
